{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AJNLgoxr8KS29EoQnpj1ZG","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd029-comm\n     2→title: \"cmd/comm: Compare Two Sorted Files Line by Line\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  macOS BSD comm lacks the --nocheck-order and --output-delimiter flags present in GNU\n     6→  comm. Scripts that compare sorted file pairs for set-intersection or set-difference\n     7→  operations depend on GNU comm behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew\n     8→  GNU reference binary (gcomm, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag\n     9→  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Read two sorted files and produce three-column output showing lines unique to file 1, unique to file 2, and common to both, matching GNU comm behavior.\n    13→  - G2: Support -1, -2, and -3 flags to suppress individual output columns.\n    14→  - G3: Support --check-order and --nocheck-order to control whether input ordering is validated.\n    15→  - G4: Support --output-delimiter to configure the column separator.\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Three-column comparison output\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: Must read file1 and file2 simultaneously, line by line. Lines present only in file1 are written in column 1 (no indentation). Lines present only in file2 are written in column 2 (one tab indent). Lines present in both are written in column 3 (two tabs indent).\n    22→      - R1.2: Must process files in sorted order; when a line from file1 is lexicographically less than the current file2 line, it is a column-1 line. When file2's line is less, it is a column-2 line. Equal lines go to column 3.\n    23→      - R1.3: When one file is exhausted, all remaining lines from the other file go to the appropriate column (column 1 if file1 has lines left, column 2 if file2 has lines left).\n    24→      - R1.4: Comparison is byte-for-byte with LC_ALL=C (no locale collation). An optional newline at the end of the last line does not affect comparison.\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Column suppression flags (-1, -2, -3)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: -1 must suppress column 1 (lines unique to file1). The remaining columns shift left in indentation.\n    30→      - R2.2: -2 must suppress column 2 (lines unique to file2). The remaining columns shift left accordingly.\n    31→      - R2.3: -3 must suppress column 3 (lines common to both files). Combining -1 -2 -3 produces no output.\n    32→      - R2.4: When a column is suppressed, the tab indentation for remaining columns adjusts so that the leftmost output column has no leading tab.\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Order checking and output delimiter\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: By default, comm checks that input files are sorted. When a line is encountered that is less than the previous line in the same file, comm prints a warning to stderr and continues.\n    38→      - R3.2: --check-order makes the ordering check fatal; comm exits non-zero if input is not sorted.\n    39→      - R3.3: --nocheck-order disables the sorting check entirely; no warning is printed even if input is out of order.\n    40→      - R3.4: --output-delimiter=STRING replaces the default tab character as the column separator. All column indentation levels use STRING instead of a tab.\n    41→\n    42→  R4:\n    43→    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    44→    items:\n    45→      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully and no order violations occur.\n    46→      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr.\n    47→      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    48→      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n    49→\n    50→non_goals:\n    51→  - cmd/comm does not sort its inputs; both files must already be sorted before invocation.\n    52→  - cmd/comm does not implement locale-aware collation; comparison is byte-for-byte under LC_ALL=C.\n    53→\n    54→acceptance_criteria:\n    55→  - \"AC1: comm on file1='a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' and file2='b\\\\nc\\\\nd\\\\n' produces the three-column output with 'a' in col1, 'd' in col2, and 'b','c' in col3, matching gcomm.\"\n    56→  - \"AC2: comm -12 on the same files produces only 'b\\\\nc\\\\n' (common lines), matching gcomm -12.\"\n    57→  - \"AC3: comm -3 on the same files produces 'a\\\\n\\\\td\\\\n' (unique lines), matching gcomm -3.\"\n    58→  - \"AC4: comm --output-delimiter=| uses | instead of tab for column separation, matching gcomm.\"\n    59→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gcomm passes for all required test cases.\"\n    60→  - \"AC6: cmd/comm compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    61→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c872d7a-5073-490d-aeb2-b9cbd293cce9","uuid":"9cbd0d6b-f545-4435-8ad7-52c9349f0089","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/358/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd029-comm.yaml","content":"id: prd029-comm\ntitle: \"cmd/comm: Compare Two Sorted Files Line by Line\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD comm lacks the --nocheck-order and --output-delimiter flags present in GNU\n  comm. Scripts that compare sorted file pairs for set-intersection or set-difference\n  operations depend on GNU comm behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew\n  GNU reference binary (gcomm, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag\n  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Read two sorted files and produce three-column output showing lines unique to file 1, unique to file 2, and common to both, matching GNU comm behavior.\n  - G2: Support -1, -2, and -3 flags to suppress individual output columns.\n  - G3: Support --check-order and --nocheck-order to control whether input ordering is validated.\n  - G4: Support --output-delimiter to configure the column separator.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Three-column comparison output\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must read file1 and file2 simultaneously, line by line. Lines present only in file1 are written in column 1 (no indentation). Lines present only in file2 are written in column 2 (one tab indent). Lines present in both are written in column 3 (two tabs indent).\n      - R1.2: Must process files in sorted order; when a line from file1 is lexicographically less than the current file2 line, it is a column-1 line. When file2's line is less, it is a column-2 line. Equal lines go to column 3.\n      - R1.3: When one file is exhausted, all remaining lines from the other file go to the appropriate column (column 1 if file1 has lines left, column 2 if file2 has lines left).\n      - R1.4: Comparison is byte-for-byte with LC_ALL=C (no locale collation). An optional newline at the end of the last line does not affect comparison.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Column suppression flags (-1, -2, -3)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -1 must suppress column 1 (lines unique to file1). The remaining columns shift left in indentation.\n      - R2.2: -2 must suppress column 2 (lines unique to file2). The remaining columns shift left accordingly.\n      - R2.3: -3 must suppress column 3 (lines common to both files). Combining -1 -2 -3 produces no output.\n      - R2.4: When a column is suppressed, the tab indentation for remaining columns adjusts so that the leftmost output column has no leading tab.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Order checking and output delimiter\n    items:\n      - R3.1: By default, comm checks that input files are sorted. When a line is encountered that is less than the previous line in the same file, comm prints a warning to stderr and continues.\n      - R3.2: --check-order makes the ordering check fatal; comm exits non-zero if input is not sorted.\n      - R3.3: --nocheck-order disables the sorting check entirely; no warning is printed even if input is out of order.\n      - R3.4: --output-delimiter=STRING replaces the default tab character as the column separator. All column indentation levels use STRING instead of a tab.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully and no order violations occur.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr.\n      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/comm does not sort its inputs; both files must already be sorted before invocation.\n  - cmd/comm does not implement locale-aware collation; comparison is byte-for-byte under LC_ALL=C.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: comm on file1='a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' and file2='b\\\\nc\\\\nd\\\\n' produces the three-column output with 'a' in col1, 'd' in col2, and 'b','c' in col3, matching gcomm.\"\n  - \"AC2: comm -12 on the same files produces only 'b\\\\nc\\\\n' (common lines), matching gcomm -12.\"\n  - \"AC3: comm -3 on the same files produces 'a\\\\n\\\\td\\\\n' (unique lines), matching gcomm -3.\"\n  - \"AC4: comm --output-delimiter=| uses | instead of tab for column separation, matching gcomm.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gcomm passes for all required test cases.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/comm compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":61,"startLine":1,"totalLines":61}}}
