ce I'll be comparing against the actual gcomm output in my differential tests, the YAML test expectations won't affect the correctness of my implementation. Now I'm looking at the --total flag, which should append a summary line showing counts for each column. I also need to handle --check-order to make unsorted input fatal, and --nocheck-order to disable the check entirely. The default behavior checks for sorted input and warns if files aren't sorted, so I'll implement that according to the spec.\n\nFor testing, since the test utilities don't exist, I'll write inline comparison logic that builds the binary, locates the reference implementation, creates temporary files with test data, runs both versions, and compares their output. Since comm reads from files rather than stdin, the tests will need to set up temp directories with the input files and pass the file paths as arguments.\n\nWriting the main implementation...\n\nFor the flag parsing, I need to handle the tricky case where `-1`, `-2`, `-3` can be combined like `-12` or `-123`, but the standard flag package will interpret `-12` as a negative number rather than two separate flags. I'll need a custom approach to parse these correctly, since GNU comm allows this syntax. Writing the preprocessing function...\n\nNow I'm implementing the core merge-comparison algorithm that reads through two sorted files simultaneously, checking for ordering violations and comparing lines based on the configuration settings. Finishing the drain loops for both readers, then I need to handle the delimiter logic for output—the number of delimiters before each column depends on how many non-suppressed columns come before it. I'll create a lookup to compute this efficiently. For the `--total` flag, I'll also need to track line counts in each column and append them at the end.\n\nNow looking at the ordering checks: there are three modes to implement. The default warns on unsorted input and continues, `--check-order` makes it fatal and exits with an error, and `--nocheck-order` skips the check entirely. GNU comm's actual behavior is a bit more subtle—it warns once per file and then stops checking—but I need to verify what the requirements actually specify.\n\nLet me implement according to the PRD and use the differential tests to validate against the real GNU comm behavior. I'll start writing the code now, aiming to keep main.go concise.\n\nWriting the order checking logic... Setting up flag parsing...\n\nI need to track whether the user explicitly set `--output-delimiter` since the behavior differs when it's provided versus when it uses the default tab character. I could use a custom flag type or check if the flag was actually passed by the user. I need to handle the --check-order and --nocheck-order flags carefully. In GNU comm, the last one specified wins, but Go's flag package will set both to true if both are provided. I'll make nocheck-order take precedence, then implement the logic: skip checking if nocheck is set, fail on unsorted input if check is set, otherwise warn by default.\n\nNow I'm implementing the order checking logic. GNU comm exits with status 1 immediately when it detects an unsorted file with --check-order, printing a diagnostic message. In default mode, it warns once per file but continues processing.\n\nWriting the implementation... I need to understand the --total flag output format: it should show counts for each column with a \"total\" label, omitting counts for suppressed columns. Let me verify this against the diff tests, then write out the complete implementation including a self-contained test file that builds the binary and runs it. Writing the test helper... Writing test structure...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the order-checking behavior—I'll implement three modes: default warns to stderr and continues, `--check-order` exits on violation, and `--nocheck-order` skips the check entirely. For the main comparison loop, I'm using a Scanner to read lines, though I need to